      "title": "RoboShield v2: an inline command firewall gateway for ROS 2 (Rust + rclrs, two-domain bridge, SROS2)",
      "link": "https://discourse.openrobotics.org/t/roboshield-v2-an-inline-command-firewall-gateway-for-ros-2-rust-rclrs-two-domain-bridge-sros2/58826",
      "summary": "Hi all, Follow-up on RoboShield, my ROS 2 security project. v1 was a passive Rust watchdog that parsed raw RTPS packets with libpcap and flagged unknown GUID prefixes, unauthorized publishers and topic floods. It could alert, but it couldn’t stop anything. v2 is an inline gateway that protects the robot. Repo: GitHub - Amin-Ahmed-G/robotshield: Embedded out-of-band NIDS for ROS 2 RTPS/DDS networks in Rust with libpcap packet inspection and topic spoofing detection. · GitHub (v1 is kept on branch roboshield-v1-nids ) Architecture Laptop ~~ WiFi ~~ NanoPi (gateway) ── Ethernet ── Robot domain 10 robot_firewall domain 20 untrusted only bridge isolated LAN One process creates two contexts with different domain IDs (rclrs / rclpy both support domain_id per context). It’s the only thing that bridges the two domains; the kernel doesn’t forward packets (ip_forward=0 , nftables default-drop, port and peer allowlists per interface). SROS2 only on the WiFi side: rcl reads ROS_SECURITY_ENABLE when each context initializes, so setting it per context gives an enforced enclave on the untrusted side and a plain domain on the robot side, for robots that can’t do DDS-Security. Every command (sensor_",
      "publishedAt": "2026-10-13T16:13:00+00:00",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-13T16:13:00+00:00",
      "source": "ROS2 Discourse",
      "section_hint": "로보틱스",
      "articleText": "RoboShield v2: an inline command firewall gateway for ROS 2 (Rust + rclrs, two-domain bridge, SROS2) - Projects - Open Robotics Discourse RoboShield v2: an inline command firewall gateway for ROS 2 (Rust + rclrs, two-domain bridge, SROS2) Projects ros2 , security , wg-rust , sros2 , jazzy Amin_Ahmed_G October 13, 2026, 4:13pm 1 Hi all, Follow-up on RoboShield, my ROS 2 security project. v1 was a passive Rust watchdog that parsed raw RTPS packets with libpcap and flagged unknown GUID prefixes, unauthorized publishers and topic floods. It could alert, but it couldn’t stop anything. v2 is an inline gateway that protects the robot. Repo: GitHub - Amin-Ahmed-G/robotshield: Embedded out-of-band NIDS for ROS 2 RTPS/DDS networks in Rust with libpcap packet inspection and topic spoofing detection. · GitHub (v1 is kept on branch roboshield-v1-nids ) Architecture Laptop ~~ WiFi ~~ NanoPi (gateway) ── Ethernet ── Robot domain 10 robot_firewall domain 20 untrusted only bridge isolated LAN One process creates two contexts with different domain IDs (rclrs / rclpy both support domain_id per context). It’s the only thing that bridges the two domains; the kernel doesn’t forward packets (ip_forward=0 , nftables default-drop, port and peer allowlists per interface). SROS2 only on the WiFi side: rcl reads ROS_SECURITY_ENABLE when each context initializes, so setting it per context gives an enforced enclave on the untrusted side and a plain domain on the robot side, for robots that can’t do DDS-Security. Every command (sensor_msgs/JointState , or Float64MultiArray for forward_command_controller ) passes through a rule engine that checks: joint names and array lengths, NaN/inf, per-joint position/velocity/effort limits (clamp or drop), sudden jumps since the last accepted command, a token-bucket rate limit, and stale or replayed header stamps. Repeated incidents, a second publisher on the robot’s command topic (a rogue device on the robot LAN), extra joint_states publishers (state spoofing), or the robot reporting a position out of bounds trigger LOCKDOWN : all input is dropped and the robot is held at its last safe pose until an operator re-arms it. It also boots locked, and the systemd unit has a watchdog. Per-message publisher filtering: each command’s MessageInfo.publisher_gid is matched to a node via the graph, so only a spoofing node’s messages are dropped and the real operator keeps control. Results (laptop, Ryzen 7 7435HS, Jazzy, Fast DDS; NanoPi numbers pending) Rule engine, per 6-joint command 1.37 µs median in Rust (Python: 9.5 µs) Latency the gateway adds end to end +0.5 ms median (Python version: +1.0 ms), 1000/1000 delivered Attack self-test, SROS2 on, a cert-less publisher spamming throughout 8/8 detected, robot never past its limit 600 msg/s flood → lockdown ~10 ms Benchmark scripts and raw data are in bench/ . Things I hit that might help others rclrs on Jazzy binaries: the message crates shipped in /opt/ros/jazzy/share/*/rust are built against rosidl_runtime_rs 0.6, so rclrs 0.7.0 works and 0.8 fails with MessageIDL trait errors. rclrs 0.7 also links against test_msgs , so ros-jazzy-test-msgs must be installed. rclrs TopicEndpointInfo doesn’t expose endpoint_gid . I vendored rclrs with a one-field patch (vendor/rclrs/PATCH.md ) so publisher GIDs can be matched to node names. rclpy (Jazzy) message info has no publisher_gid , so the Python gateway can’t attribute individual messages and falls back to locking down when an unknown publisher appears. Discovery race: DDS can deliver data before the ROS node info for that endpoint arrives, so the publisher shows up as _NODE_NAME_UNKNOWN_ . The gateway fails closed but gives new publishers a 2 s grace period before raising an alarm. Questions for the community Would an endpoint_gid field on TopicEndpointInfo be welcome upstream in rclrs? Happy to open a PR. Is there interest in exposing publisher_gid in rclpy’s message info? In Fast DDS, what’s the cleanest way to pin each participant in one process to its own interface? Right now I rely on nftables for that. Has anyone shipped least-privilege SROS2 policy files for a teleop/commander enclave they’d recommend as a template? A note on v1: my earlier post described inline blocking. That was the plan, but v1 shipped as alert-only; v2 is where enforcement actually lives. Feedback on the threat model and the lockdown semantics is very welcome, especially from anyone running ros2_control over untrusted networks. Thanks, Amin 1 Like Related topics Topic Replies Views Activity RoboShield: A Low-Latency Out-of-Band RTPS Watchdog in Rust ROS General ros2 , dds , wg-security , security 0 143 July 13, 2026 Expose secure communication only with specified entities ROS General ros2 , security , humble 5 439 December 5, 2024 SROS2: Usable Cyber Security Tools for ROS 2 ROS General ros2 , wg-security , security 0 1777 March 17, 2022 Open Class - Security in ROS2 Training & Education ros2 0 1124 May 4, 2023 ROS2 behind a firewall ROS General ros2 3 5949 April 25, 2023",
